## Architecture Impact

- architecture_relevance: relevant
- architecture_impact: high
- architecture_reason: A qualified child-generation switch changes concurrency, resource routing and recovery across the launcher/STDIO/server/view boundaries. Existing public recording/approval contracts, canonical current state and structured closeout also require shared design and compatibility. File counts are irrelevant to this decision.
- architecture_evidence: Existing-System View; EVIDENCE_BROWNFIELD_RENEWED_03.md; UR AC-014..016; actual launcher spawn/exit path and server/session owners
- architecture_missing_evidence: Native capability loss at the actual Codex connection is not located. Existing launcher controls one child but has no generation broker or safe replacement operation. PRD must state supported/dependency-blocked outcomes; SD must resolve generation admission/routing/close confirmation before TP. These are mandatory delivery prerequisites, not missing facts about full-depth impact.
- architecture_next_owner_and_action: AGDF launchPluginMcpServer owner for only directly spawned children in one invocation; existing server/executor/session owners for active work; external Codex host for capability forwarding and teardown. PRD names these support boundaries; SD designs the own-child extension and required capability checks. Missing host support remains an explicit external dependency and blocks affected native acceptance.

## Reuse And Parallel-Structure Risk

| Finding | Evidence | Risk | Required action |
|---|---|---|---|
| problem: previous coverage descriptions are stale | Current source now uses prepareRunContentState, typed next_operation and structured closeout; EVIDENCE_BROWNFIELD_RENEWED_03.md | revise | Renew PRD/SD around retained implementation and remaining qualifications; do not recreate repaired owners or transfer old TP fulfillment |
| problem: runtime ownership is narrower than package ownership | plugin-runtime.js owns one child handle and shared package directories; server.js owns one STDIO composition | revise | Extend the existing per-connection launch owner; generation identity must derive from direct creation/ownership. Shared dataRoot, PID/name/repository/age cannot prove process ownership |
| problem: current launcher has no generation routing/drain | spawn with inherited STDIO; finite executor and view sessions are distinct | revise | SD must design the bounded own-child admission/routing seam, preserve existing protocol traffic and qualify it before enabling replacement. A shared global supervisor or business-policy router is excluded |
| problem: directory retirement can remove a runtime still used by another connection | retireRuntimeRoot/pruneMcpDataRoot rename/delete owned package directories without live-child claims | revise | SD must distinguish immutable executable-generation retention from process retirement and coordinate the existing package owner; protect foreign connections. No accepted debt or age-based file/process deletion substitute |
| problem: unsupported native UI is externally owned | Actual native host_ui_unsupported/resource_denied; isolated real server supports explicitly declared UI | revise | External Codex host owns capability propagation. AGDF preserves actual declarations and rejects unavailable UI. The approved pool does not claim to repair this separate cause; no implementation/QA success for AC-016 until actual host proof |
| problem: unknown effect and active sessions survive generation change | Existing operation UUID/receipt checks; decision-session pending/readPending and private epoch | revise | Reuse canonical UUID/status/recovery; pin traffic and sessions to original child; no automatic write replay, eviction or migrated view authority |
| problem: overlapping active runs and retained tests | Current exact reference inventory in renewed evidence | warn | Preserve all foreign Run bytes/approvals; current Run owns renewed integrated contract plus bounded lifecycle extension. Recheck source/worktree overlap after renewed TP |

No retained-debt trade-off is accepted. The external support dependency is explicit, not a promise to work around it or silently reduce acceptance. Missing design/qualification remains owned downstream work. This pass decides sizing and reuse; it does not assert that the existing launcher already implements safe replacement or that native support is fixed.

